WEBVTT

00:00:00.655 --> 00:00:30.111
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. It's Wednesday, and this stretch of the week is all about Maker and Repair. Today we've got Apple's HomeView hub, which iFixit tore down and found glued shut with parts ripped getting in. There's a Massachusetts seventh grader's gravity-powered solar tracker that cost under $25, and a Creality printer turned into a bioprinter. Then the deep dive asks what you're still allowed to fix when the company would rather you bought another. Stay with us.

00:00:43.586 --> 00:01:06.802
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Wednesday, November 18th, 2026. Today's theme is Repair Culture &amp; Practical Tech, the gear you can open, fix and keep running. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:07.802 --> 00:01:31.970
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're asking what you're still allowed to fix when the company that built it would rather you bought another one. Out at Horsefly Lake it's overcast and minus 3 this morning, with a high of 2, a low of minus 3, and fog through the day. 100 Mile House is minus 5 and clear, Williams Lake is minus 3 in fog, Quesnel is minus 2 and clear, and Anahim Lake is minus 7 in fog, so watch the road into Williams Lake.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:33.018
<v Riley>On to the news.

00:01:34.118 --> 00:01:42.382
<v Casey>A lot of today's news sits close to home, starting with council spending and warm water off the B.C. coast. The rest is people opening up gear and rebuilding it.

00:01:44.026 --> 00:02:18.256
<v Casey>Williams Lake council has a late agenda item seeking approval of an estimated $1,740.19 in taxpayer-funded costs for Mayor Surinderpal Rathor and Councillor Scott Nelson to travel to Victoria. They would attend the swearing-in of Premier-Designate Lorne Doerkson on November 20th. The item covers meals, travel and accommodation. Most councillors voiced support for the trip, but some raised concerns about how the decision was made. Councillor Michael Moses said relationship building with government has been valuable to the city, and the mayor called it a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity.

00:02:18.706 --> 00:02:22.178
<v Riley>The late addition is the part that drew the process concerns.

00:02:23.792 --> 00:02:52.280
<v Riley>Ocean Networks Canada says deep-water temperatures off the B.C. coast have hit records. Its Barkley Canyon site, about 400 metres below the surface, logged 8.21 degrees Celsius on November 8th, nearly 2.5 degrees above that site's long-term average. ONC president Jay Cullen says the warming runs through a large part of the water column. The agency links it to a Super El Niño building now, and says the site is still running warm for mid-November.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:58.514
<v Casey>Somebody has to keep sensors running four hundred metres down. That's a maintenance bill nobody reads in the headline.

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:28.792
<v Casey>South Cariboo crafters are selling handmade gifts at the South Cariboo Farmers Market, 108 Heritage Market, Forest Grove Family Market, and the Christmas markets this time of year. Amber Tetlock, who runs Homemade Treasures in the Cariboo, started selling homemade stuffed animals a little over a year and a half ago. She moved to the South Cariboo four and a half years ago from Abbotsford, and says the lifestyle has given her sons room to get out and play. For her, a hobby became a small home business.

00:03:30.254 --> 00:04:02.908
<v Riley>We covered the tribunal's X ruling on Monday, and today's piece is about what came after. B.C.'s Civil Resolution Tribunal declined to add a second $100,000 penalty. X had geoblocked intimate images so they can't be viewed in Canada, instead of removing them. The tribunal accepted that X could not enforce B.C. law outside Canada, and that X made a reasonable attempt to comply. The report says geoblocking does not adhere to the letter of the law. The B.C. legislature passed the Intimate Images Protection Act in 2023.

00:04:03.358 --> 00:04:07.590
<v Casey>The image still exists everywhere outside Canada, and the tribunal couldn't reach it.

00:04:09.244 --> 00:04:34.788
<v Casey>Nabu Casa's Home Assistant Connect AUX-2 costs $69, and it's a box for older gear. It has RS-232 serial for older TVs and other equipment, and it connects older stereos and amplifiers to modern multi-room audio. The pitch is that an old device shouldn't be thrown out when its cloud service gets switched off. Paulus Schoutsen, founder of Home Assistant and Nabu Casa, put it this way: "The most sustainable device is the one you already own."

00:04:36.287 --> 00:05:04.679
<v Riley>Boing Boing takes the other side of that shelf. The post argues that the Digital Millennium Copyright Act makes it a crime to remove encryption from software. That means you can't customize or repair encrypted devices without breaking the law. Boing Boing says manufacturers use that protection to encrypt invasive spyware on consumer electronics, including TVs. Its headline calls it a dumb copyright law, and says it's illegal to remove the spyware from your own TV.

00:05:05.129 --> 00:05:07.985
<v Casey>So the owner needs permission to change their own TV.

00:05:09.777 --> 00:05:39.511
<v Casey>Hydra Manufacturing is a University of Leeds spin-out in the UK. Its chief executive, Louis Masters, started on the problem during his doctorate: most 3D printers can't repair a defect once it has happened. The Leeds machine carries a milling spindle beside its extruder. If a layer comes up short, it can cut back to the previous layer, rewind the print instructions, and try again. Masters labelled thousands of images by hand, drawing boxes around too much paste or too little, so the machine could learn to spot faults.

00:05:39.961 --> 00:05:44.993
<v Riley>The company sold its first machine a week after it was incorporated. Demand came fast.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:06:13.198
<v Riley>iFixit tore down Apple's HomeView, a seven-inch display on a HomePod mini, and called it a surprising step backward. Recent iPads and HomePods had been getting easier to open, with easier battery access, replaceable USB-C ports, and official repair manuals for the iPad. HomeView came apart with a few screws and a lot of strong adhesive. iFixit says the device was damaged during disassembly, and that its team ripped parts getting in.

00:06:13.648 --> 00:06:16.960
<v Casey>Skilled techs broke parts on it. That's the detail I'd remember.

00:06:18.612 --> 00:06:36.236
<v Casey>Inheritance Machining built a universal, self-centering tap wrench from stock, based on a design posted on Reddit by another maker. One screw through one of four inserts sets the size of the hole the tap fits into. A commenter suggested adding a support on the other side of the adjustment screw for more accuracy.

00:06:37.833 --> 00:07:05.369
<v Riley>Jason Chang is a 13-year-old seventh grader at Worcester Academy in Massachusetts. He got tired of moving his family's solar Christmas lights to chase the sun, so he built a tracker that tilts a panel with dripping water and gravity. It needs no motor, sensor, or electricity. The prototype cost less than $25. In outdoor tests it generated about 20 percent more voltage than a fixed panel, according to CBS Boston's report.

00:07:05.818 --> 00:07:08.794
<v Casey>One prototype, tested outdoors. That's the evidence so far.

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:25.900
<v Casey>Citizen Lab and Confiant linked the Israeli ad tech firm Discover-Tech to suspected surveillance through online advertising. Its ads appeared across nearly 200,000 websites and mobile apps. The report ties the firm to a suspected Starlink tracking effort.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:53.308
<v Riley>Matt Williams pulled an old Fortinet FortiGate 60 firewall out of the trash. Its VIA C3 board runs at 400 megahertz, and the 128 megabytes of memory are soldered on. He had to reverse-engineer the original firmware just to bring the memory up. With that done, Linux 2.4 booted, and DOOM ran through the framebuffer. The screen shows green because only the green pin on the VGA connector was probed.

00:07:53.758 --> 00:07:56.638
<v Casey>Someone threw out a firewall that could still run Linux.

00:07:58.371 --> 00:08:11.459
<v Casey>PancakeSlicer is a free, self-hostable slicer for non-planar printing, where the print path curves along all three axes instead of stacking flat layers. Tom's Hardware says it may make that kind of printing more accessible to hobbyists.

00:08:12.935 --> 00:08:36.631
<v Riley>On an Intel Arc A750, a local AI setup ran 19 to 22 tokens per second under Ollama. Switching to a Vulkan-based llama.cpp build reached around 33, and 35 for lighter models after a setting change. Tokens are the pieces a model writes out, so higher numbers mean faster answers. The writer found Ollama's management layer added overhead on the same card.

00:08:37.081 --> 00:08:40.753
<v Casey>Same move as the firewall. New software on hardware you already own.

00:08:42.433 --> 00:09:02.153
<v Casey>Kathy Korevec built ZigZag, a free, open-source embroidery editor for Mac and Windows, after her $300 Brother Skitch machine wanted a subscription. ZigZag turns SVG artwork into stitches and exports the formats most embroidery machines read. It can also send designs straight to the Skitch over Bluetooth, using a protocol that was reverse-engineered.

00:09:03.626 --> 00:09:16.394
<v Riley>How-To Geek says GeoWorks Ensemble beat Windows 3.0 in most areas, but it never took much of the PC market. The reasons given are too few third-party apps, and GeoWorks' view that Microsoft refused it a fair fight.

00:09:16.843 --> 00:09:19.611
<v Casey>Platforms die on the app count. That's an old story.

00:09:21.316 --> 00:09:42.100
<v Casey>Adam Turaj built Studprint, a working 3D printer that is about 92 percent LEGO parts. The frame, the bed, and the motion parts are bricks, with LEGO lead screws driving the axes. The custom mounts and printing hardware have open-source CAD files anyone can copy and change. It's slow and small, and its unheated ABS brick bed limits which materials it can print.

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:45.342
<v Riley>The open CAD files matter more than the bricks.

00:09:47.057 --> 00:10:09.465
<v Riley>In the UK, replacing smart meters could cost up to £4 billion, because network upgrades mean many of them will need replacing soon. TechRadar's headline is that the meters might not have been such a smart idea after all. Devices in our homes and shops, and whether their owners are allowed to open them, is where the deep dive picks up. First, a word about a local organization.

00:10:10.565 --> 00:10:33.573
<v Casey>School District 27, Cariboo-Chilcotin, runs public schools across Williams Lake, 100 Mile House, and the surrounding Cariboo communities, with rural education programs built around the places students actually live. If you have a child in its schools, or you're thinking about enrolling one, the district's website is sd27.bc.ca. Ask what's being taught this year, and ask who is teaching it.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.480
<v Riley>Schools are where a lot of practical skill gets passed on, so this one is worth a look.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:40.244
<v Casey>Into the deep dive.

00:10:41.344 --> 00:11:00.440
<v Riley>Take a company with a good repair record and a new product on the shelf. Should a buyer expect the new one to open as easily as the old ones did? Apple is the test this week. Its iPads got easier to open. The second-generation HomePod could be opened without wrecking it. Then came HomeView, the hub iFixit tore down.

00:11:01.059 --> 00:11:09.403
<v Casey>And the record didn't carry over. iFixit's skilled techs ripped parts getting it apart, and The Verge read that as Apple's repair progress being abandoned.

00:11:09.916 --> 00:11:20.356
<v Riley>Thin glued assemblies are how you get a slim hub. That's a design trade. iFixit's own line is the sharp part: designing for repair is an intentional act, and here, Apple didn't bother.

00:11:20.966 --> 00:11:35.294
<v Casey>Intentional, and the glue ships in the box. Nobody can point to a number either. iFixit has no official repairability scorecard for this device category. Shahram Mokhtari joked he'd give it a "BEEP this thing" out of 10. That was the joke. It isn't a score.

00:11:35.867 --> 00:11:44.099
<v Riley>So the verdict is a judgment, and it's a public one. Somebody opened the thing, showed the work, and the next buyer can read what's inside.

00:11:44.699 --> 00:11:51.975
<v Casey>After the box is sold. HomeView is $299 in the U.S. and went on sale November 13th. Nobody reads the adhesive before paying.

00:11:51.975 --> 00:12:02.223
<v Riley>Which is the gap. A teardown is a free check, and it lands in the same week as the product. The fix is a buyer who reads it before paying, and that's a habit, not a policy.

00:12:02.739 --> 00:12:14.539
<v Casey>A habit needs time and a reason. A household in Williams Lake or Quesnel gets the hub from a store shelf, not from a teardown report. And a teardown comes from one team, on one afternoon, with one set of tools.

00:12:15.162 --> 00:12:25.866
<v Riley>The teardown did its job, though. iFixit documented the damage in detail, with the photos to back it. That's the teardown ecosystem working. Most products never get opened in public at all.

00:12:26.472 --> 00:12:34.088
<v Casey>It got reported. Whether anyone at Apple changes the next design because of it is a different question, and nothing in the coverage answers it.

00:12:34.663 --> 00:12:42.391
<v Riley>Apple has done repair engineering before. The HomeView is a regression, not proof the company can't build a repairable product.

00:12:43.016 --> 00:12:49.824
<v Casey>A company that can do it and chose not to is a different risk from one that can't. The buyer can't tell which one they bought.

00:12:50.125 --> 00:12:53.989
<v Riley>The buyer can tell from the teardown. That's what the teardown is for.

00:12:54.634 --> 00:13:02.650
<v Casey>The teardown tells you about the screws and the glue. It doesn't tell you what the company intended, and it doesn't tell you whether the next model will be better.

00:13:03.242 --> 00:13:11.986
<v Riley>Then there's the part the screwdriver can't reach. That Boing Boing post on the copyright law means some devices are closed by statute, not adhesive.

00:13:12.658 --> 00:13:17.178
<v Casey>So the lid is shut by a copyright rule as well as a screw. That's a longer repair.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:26.560
<v Riley>It's slower, and it's a policy lever. A statute can be amended. A glued case can only be pried open, and the prying is how you get the parts broken.

00:13:27.205 --> 00:13:37.373
<v Casey>Amended by people who have been persuaded to amend it. Boing Boing's headline is about a TV. That's one shelf. The change has to show up in what gets sold, not only in the headline.

00:13:37.924 --> 00:14:11.826
<v Riley>Now look at the other end of the spectrum. University of Rochester microbiologists modified a Creality Ender 3, a hobby printer that costs about $450, into a bioprinter with a custom syringe pump. Tom's Hardware's headline says it outperforms machines costing more than $100,000. Hackster quotes the lab saying the parts are easy to get, and the instructions are on GitHub, which hosts this show's code too. Earlier DIY bioprinters were limited to structures under about 20 layers. This one prints more than 100.

00:14:12.411 --> 00:14:29.235
<v Casey>Read the paper, not the headline. The paper calls it less expensive than many commercial bioprinters. That isn't outperforming all of them. The comparison is self-reported, and I couldn't find an independent replication. The living-materials result also depended on new materials, not only the printer.

00:14:29.883 --> 00:14:42.571
<v Riley>The headline oversells it, and the public files still count. A lab that couldn't buy a commercial machine can build one from a set of instructions. Open designs are a public good even when the press release is loud.

00:14:43.132 --> 00:14:48.680
<v Casey>The files are a real gain. It's a lab win, though. It doesn't tell a household it can open a sealed hub.

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:56.656
<v Riley>Mm-hm. Nobody is pretending a hub is a bioprinter. The transferable part is the method: common parts, a public design, and a skill you can learn.

00:14:57.249 --> 00:15:12.313
<v Casey>Then the transfer depends on someone learning the skill. On September 9th we asked whether 3D printing teaches the underlying skills or replaces them. The bioprinter is the same question one step further along. The lab had to know the machine well enough to change it.

00:15:12.978 --> 00:15:23.658
<v Riley>Some repair is designed in from the start, like that Leeds printer with the milling spindle that backs up and retries a short layer. It's built so the machine fixes its own faults.

00:15:24.267 --> 00:15:35.435
<v Casey>Designed in by the company, which makes it a feature. If the vendor stops supporting the software, who keeps that feature working? That's the question I'd put to Hydra, and I'd want the answer in writing.

00:15:35.955 --> 00:15:42.731
<v Riley>A first sale a week after incorporation tells you there's a market for a printer that fixes its own mistakes.

00:15:43.398 --> 00:15:52.014
<v Casey>A market for the first machine. Somebody pays for that labelling work when the machine learns a new fault. The coverage doesn't say whether the buyer does.

00:15:52.625 --> 00:16:10.809
<v Riley>The tank is the human end of it. Simon Sörensen is an RC vehicle expert. He took the RC idea of steering by giving one side more power, and printed a rideable tank around it. When it works, it crosses loose sand quite well. The track design is still being iterated, and a belt tensioner is being added.

00:16:11.416 --> 00:16:18.328
<v Casey>When it works is the whole sentence. Loose sand is the test that separates a demo from a machine you'd take out on a weekend.

00:16:18.887 --> 00:16:29.571
<v Riley>Iteration in public is how a skill gets built. The RC knowledge carried over to a new machine, and every failed track teaches the next version. Nothing is wasted when one design doesn't hold.

00:16:29.571 --> 00:16:36.299
<v Casey>Ha. The skill stays with whoever did the iterating. It teaches the builder. Whether it teaches the town is a separate question.

00:16:36.978 --> 00:16:44.674
<v Riley>Then look at what a town can do with the same logic. That solar tracker from Massachusetts has no motor, no sensor and no electricity.

00:16:45.236 --> 00:16:54.204
<v Casey>One prototype, outdoors, in one test. That's where the number stops. The article doesn't say how it held up over a season, and a Cariboo winter is a long season.

00:16:54.737 --> 00:17:06.585
<v Riley>Winter is a design constraint. Water freezes. A drip system needs a heated reservoir or a different fluid. That's a known engineering problem with a known workaround, not a verdict against the idea.

00:17:07.216 --> 00:17:14.856
<v Casey>Maybe. Then it's a hobby with a heater bolted to it. Who maintains the heater in year five, and who replaces the reservoir when it cracks?

00:17:15.198 --> 00:17:22.814
<v Riley>Whoever is in the shop that year. That's the point of making it local. A student built this at home, and a school can build the next one.

00:17:23.384 --> 00:17:30.096
<v Casey>The student built the first one. The school still has to budget for the second and the third, and the article doesn't mention a budget.

00:17:30.644 --> 00:17:39.980
<v Riley>Software shows the same move. That trashed firewall only booted Linux because Matt Williams reverse-engineered its firmware to bring up the soldered memory.

00:17:40.543 --> 00:17:49.367
<v Casey>A firewall on a 400 megahertz board is not what you run for a school network. Use it for learning, and keep the real network on something with support.

00:17:50.031 --> 00:17:55.679
<v Riley>The useful part is that a thrown-out device still boots. The skill was the part nobody else had.

00:17:55.979 --> 00:17:59.579
<v Casey>The skill walked out the door with one person, which is my whole concern.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:15.996
<v Riley>Same card, different software on the Arc A750: local AI went from about 19 to 22 tokens per second up to around 33. The writer found Ollama's management layer added overhead, and its default context window was 4,096 tokens.

00:18:16.669 --> 00:18:23.885
<v Casey>A default setting, not the card. Vendors ship defaults that work for the demo, and the buyer finds out later which settings matter.

00:18:24.475 --> 00:18:32.459
<v Riley>Kathy Korevec's embroidery machine shows it too. The subscription was the trigger. The reverse-engineered Bluetooth protocol is the workaround.

00:18:33.089 --> 00:18:40.761
<v Casey>The subscription is the lever the vendor pulls, and the protocol is the lever the buyer pulls back. Who is on the hook when the vendor changes the protocol?

00:18:41.115 --> 00:18:45.443
<v Riley>Whoever maintains the repository. The code is public, so anyone can pick it up.

00:18:45.767 --> 00:18:50.175
<v Casey>Anyone who can read the code, in other words. That's a short list in most towns.

00:18:50.764 --> 00:18:59.052
<v Riley>Then the list grows when a school shop class can read it. The LEGO printer's open CAD files are the same idea, and they matter more than the bricks.

00:18:59.697 --> 00:19:05.201
<v Casey>The bricks are the hook. The printer is a demo. The CAD files are useful only if somebody can run them.

00:19:05.478 --> 00:19:07.038
<v Riley>That's the lesson for a shop.

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:15.998
<v Casey>Then consider the other side of the ledger. Those UK smart meters need replacing at a cost of up to £4 billion, and the headline doesn't say who pays that bill.

00:19:16.541 --> 00:19:24.541
<v Riley>A utility-scale replacement is a planning problem. Planning is solvable, and the planning starts with an inventory of what's installed.

00:19:25.094 --> 00:19:32.838
<v Casey>An inventory is a list. It doesn't pay for anything. And the people who wind up footing the bill are seldom in the room when the list gets written.

00:19:33.491 --> 00:19:42.251
<v Riley>So the Cariboo version is concrete. A household in 100 Mile House or Quesnel buys a hub, and the only thing it has is the box and the teardown.

00:19:42.915 --> 00:19:48.307
<v Casey>The box and a teardown that came from somewhere else. A log kept nearby doesn't repair what's sealed.

00:19:48.820 --> 00:20:11.668
<v Riley>It changes what gets bought. A school shop class or a community repair night keeps a simple log for each device: opened cleanly, glued shut, parts broken. School District 27 runs the public schools in Williams Lake, 100 Mile House and the surrounding communities, and a shop class sits inside that system. A buyer checks the log against the teardowns before paying.

00:20:12.199 --> 00:20:22.023
<v Casey>Then who keeps it running in year five? Logs die when the person who started them moves on. Nobody budgets for the spreadsheet, and a shop teacher's time is already spoken for.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:37.358
<v Riley>Tie it to something that outlasts one person. A shop class has tools, students who rotate in every year, and a reason to log what comes through the door. Amber Tetlock's hobby-turned-business is the kind of local skill base a log can lean on.

00:20:38.021 --> 00:20:46.117
<v Casey>Her skill is sewing toys. A hub is a different trade. A skill base in one trade doesn't cover another, and the log only works if the trade is in the room.

00:20:46.766 --> 00:20:53.430
<v Riley>Enough logs showing glued shut and broken parts become a buying signal. Buying signals reach product teams.

00:20:53.986 --> 00:21:01.298
<v Casey>Maybe. A handful of households in the Cariboo is not a buying signal. I can't see that from here, and I'd want evidence before I believed it.

00:21:01.894 --> 00:21:17.342
<v Riley>The open question stays on the table. When the next product regresses, who should be answerable for saying so before people buy: the teardown specialists, the maker, or a local group keeping its own record? And if the device is sealed, what can that record change?

00:21:17.607 --> 00:21:28.935
<v Casey>It changes the buyer. Whether it changes the company is the part I'd want proof of. If you have a correction, a story tip, or a repair night you want to start, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:21:29.273 --> 00:21:31.745
<v Riley>Bring a screwdriver, and have a good Wednesday.

00:21:32.344 --> 00:21:51.312
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.